{
  "comment": [
    "The menu workflow cases: scripts in the player's menu script grammar, the export each runs in, and the baseline that records what it reported.",
    "",
    "A case runs once for every design in desktop/designs.json and every palette it names (\"*\" is every palette desktop/designs.json declares). Its result is keyed design/palette/name in its group's baseline.",
    "A case takes `palettes` from its group unless it names its own. Its export is `export`, then the design's own `export`, then its group's `export`, then the settings of the menu shot it names (scripts/fixtures/menu-shots.json), then its own `export`.",
    "Its script is its own `script`, or its shot's; a script given per design is looked up by design. A step \"@name\" is that design's sequence of the name. `end` is appended to every script.",
    "A case that `continues` another starts from the game data that case left, as the next launch of the same game does; every other case starts from none.",
    "A checkpoint taken with the menu open has something focused; a case lists in `unfocused` any checkpoint where nothing focused is what the player sees.",
    "",
    "`python3 scripts/test.py workflows` replays every case headlessly (desktop/src-tauri/tests/menu_workflows.rs). `python3 scripts/test.py workflows-native` launches the exported player for the `launched` cases only.",
    "`frame` is the drawable the launched player recorded these at: a 960x600 window on a display of two pixels per point. `game` is the generated Mega Drive cartridge every case exports.",
    "`persisted` names the files in the game's data that are recorded, as patterns under that directory; a path into the menu is written as it is inside the app, under `menuAssets`.",
    "`headless` says what only a launched player knows, and why; both runners compare, and the headless one records, those fields reduced to whether they are there. Nothing is drawn headlessly: a picture is the launched runner's alone, and the states scope photographs the menu.",
    "`launched` cases are the ones workflows-native launches: RetroArch's own bind list, capture timer and state task. A scripted launch keeps the console moving (the launcher writes pause_nonactive false for any run with ROMINABOX_MENU_SHOT), so PLAY IN BACKGROUND reads ON there: a launched case takes no checkpoint on a screen that shows it."
  ],
  "frame": {"width": 1920, "height": 1200},
  "end": ["report:final"],
  "game": {"discs": 1},
  "menuAssets": "Contents/Resources/menu-assets",
  "persisted": ["volume.cfg", "background-play.cfg", "controls.cfg", "shader-choice", "remaps/**/*.rmp"],
